-| LI.12c | p:q1 | the quotient may contain a finite normal subgroup, a normal Kazhdan subgroup, or an orbit configuration to which the preceding criteria apply, so $\operatorname{Res}_{\mathrm{MF}}(E)$ is not determined here | `ManuscriptNonMF.IsReductionQuotient`; `ManuscriptNonMF.exists_reductionQuotient_with_all_three_configurations`; `ManuscriptNonMF.reduction_does_not_exclude_finiteNormalSubgroup`; `ManuscriptNonMF.reduction_does_not_exclude_normalKazhdanSubgroup`; `ManuscriptNonMF.reduction_does_not_exclude_orbitConfiguration`; `ManuscriptNonMF.reduction_does_not_determine_residual` | MISSING | MISSING | - | open | - | NO | NEW 2026-08-17, the closing sentence of Question 1 and the reason the question is a question.  Nothing is claimed and nothing could be: the sentence asserts only that the two reductions leave the last group unconstrained, which LI.11 and LI.12 establish, and that a further datum is not excluded.  The three criteria it names are `thm:criterion`, `thm:normal-kazhdan` and `thm:projection-collapse`, graded at FN.*, NK.* and CO.18--CO.22  **AUTHORED 2026-08-19, GRADE HELD.**  `Manuscript/NonMF/QuestionOneOpenness` states the sentence in the only shape it has: *not excluded* is a non-implication, and a non-implication is refuted by a witness.  `IsReductionQuotient w S q` bundles the two conclusions the sentence leans on -- a surjection killing the distinguished word and killing the normal closure of the defect generators, which is LI.11 and LI.12 exactly -- and the three configurations are transcribed by their *group-side* datum only (`HasFiniteNormalSubgroup`, `HasNormalKazhdanSubgroup`, `HasOrbitConfiguration`).  `thm:projection-collapse`'s projection is deliberately not part of the third: it lives in the target algebra and a quotient does not contain a projection.  Each predicate carries `≠ ⊥` for the reason LI.14 records.  The witness is the product reduction `sndHom_isReductionQuotient` -- put the word and the defect generators in one factor and project onto the other -- so the reduction conclusions hold by construction while the target is arbitrary, and it is not the degenerate `w = 1` instance: `prod_mark_ne_one` keeps the word and the generator nonidentity in the source.  At a two-element target all three configurations hold at once, by `⊤` and `hasKazhdanPropertyT_of_finite`.  `reduction_does_not_determine_residual` is the closing clause "so what Res_MF(E) is remains open": the same reduction data are consistent with a target of trivial genuine-corona radical and with one of nontrivial radical -- the second is the manuscript's own sign-free quotient at `LiteralSignFreeQuotient.actualCoronaMFResidual_ne_bot` -- so the two reductions compute an equation and not a value.  **Nothing in the module is about `(E/⟨w⟩)/D_coll` and nothing constructs a configuration inside it**, which is the exact strength of the printed "may still".  The grade cells stay `MISSING` on the TR.05 rule: the module is in the root import closure but no green `Build and audit` covers it yet, and "committed on main" is not "elaborated".  This row moves to EXACT on the first green run that contains the module, and not before.  As at LI.11, LI.12 and LI.12a, the declarations were authored by the reading that grades them |
 | LI.12a | p:q2 | the Clifford construction of \ref{thm:kazhdan-clifford} cannot produce such a group: it imposes $c^2 = 1$, so $\hat{c}^2 = 1$, and a torsion-free quotient $q$ then has $q(\hat{c}) = 1$ and $q(N_{\mathrm{conj}}) = 1$ | `ManuscriptNonMF.cliffordTorsionFreeObstruction`; `ManuscriptNonMF.map_eq_one_of_sq_eq_one_of_isMulTorsionFree`; `ManuscriptNonMF.map_eq_one_of_mem_normalClosure`; `conj_sq_eq_one` | EXACT | EXACT | literal | unconditional | - | NO | **REWRITTEN then REGRADED on 2026-08-17, in three moves.**  (i) The old claim — "the trivial quotient satisfies all five of the other conditions, whatever the underlying data" — described the six-condition routing-data Question 2 that this revision DELETED, so the row was a ghost: `exists_subsingleton_quotient` is badged nowhere (verified, zero occurrences) and the row's long cross-gate note argued about a `Deps` cell on text that no longer exists.  (ii) The sentence printed in its place had no counterpart, so `Manuscript/NonMF/QuestionAtoms.lean` was written to state it, and was held `MISSING` while it sat outside the root import closure.  (iii) It is now in the closure and elaborated green (`ce9ea2ff`), so the grade follows.  `conj_sq_eq_one` is the printed "it imposes `c² = 1`, so `d² = 1`", being `(a d a⁻¹)² = 1` from `d² = 1` read at `d := c`, `a := t`.  `cliffordTorsionFreeObstruction` is the rest in one statement: for an involution `d`, a set of commutators `[d, a]`, and a homomorphism to a torsion-free group, `q d = 1` and `q` kills the whole normal closure.  `def:pattern` defines `N_conj` as exactly such a normal closure, of `[d, ι(λ)]` for `λ ∈ Λ`, so phrasing the hypothesis as "every generator is a commutator with `d`" instead of naming `ι` is the printed generality and not a substitution.  **One thing a reader weighing this grade should know: the two `QuestionAtoms` declarations were written by this audit, for this row.**  They were authored to state a printed sentence that had no counterpart, not located by searching, so the same reading that grades them also produced them.  `conj_sq_eq_one` is not mine and is the independent half |
